# Error Handling Rules

## Overview

BitTasker implements comprehensive error handling across all layers of the application, from user interface to blockchain operations. This document outlines centralized error management patterns, error boundaries, and user feedback systems.

## Core Principles

### Error-First Design

- **Expect errors** - design for failure scenarios
- **Graceful degradation** - maintain functionality when possible
- **User-friendly messages** - provide actionable feedback
- **Comprehensive logging** - capture context for debugging

### Error Classification

- **User Errors**: Invalid input, permission denied, validation failures
- **System Errors**: Network failures, service unavailability, timeouts
- **Application Errors**: Logic errors, state inconsistencies, bugs
- **External Errors**: Third-party service failures, blockchain network issues

## Error Types and Interfaces

### Error Type Definitions

```typescript
// Base error interface
interface BaseError {
  code: string;
  message: string;
  timestamp: Date;
  context?: Record<string, any>;
  stack?: string;
}

// User errors
interface UserError extends BaseError {
  type: 'USER_ERROR';
  field?: string;
  validation?: Record<string, string>;
}

// System errors
interface SystemError extends BaseError {
  type: 'SYSTEM_ERROR';
  service?: string;
  retryable: boolean;
  statusCode?: number;
}

// Application errors
interface ApplicationError extends BaseError {
  type: 'APPLICATION_ERROR';
  component?: string;
  action?: string;
}

// External errors
interface ExternalError extends BaseError {
  type: 'EXTERNAL_ERROR';
  service: string;
  endpoint?: string;
  retryable: boolean;
}

type AppError = UserError | SystemError | ApplicationError | ExternalError;
```

